WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:05.411
The new "Loki" series on Disney Plus starring
Tom Hiddleston is setting out to explore where

00:00:05.411 --> 00:00:10.269
the God of Mischief disappeared to when he
grabbed the Tesseract during "Avengers: Endgame."

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:13.230
Scratching your head after the show's premiere
episode?

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:16.900
Here's the end of "Loki," episode one, explained.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.980
From the jump, "Loki" is making something
very clear: This will be an in-depth dive

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.570
into what makes the God of Mischief tick.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:27.509
This is the version of the character who was
apprehended just after the events of "The

00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:31.539
Avengers," after all, so he hasn't had the
huge redemption arc through "Thor: The Dark

00:00:31.539 --> 00:00:34.670
World," "Thor: Ragnarok" and "Avengers: Infinity
War."

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:39.430
"It's been a very long day, and I think I've
had my fill of idiots in armored suits telling

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.430
me what to do."

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:45.141
So, when the Time Variance Authority picks
Loki up in the Gobi Desert in Mongolia, he's

00:00:45.141 --> 00:00:47.310
not exactly cooperative.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:51.260
Which makes it pretty fun when one of the
Hunters knocks him out in slow-motion, before

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.850
resetting the timeline with a device called
a reset charge and carting him off to the

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.370
TVA's headquarters.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:02.370
When Loki arrives at the TVA's offices, he's
constantly trying to fight them off and escape.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:07.679
His selfish, manipulative nature bleeds through
every single conversation he has, no matter

00:01:07.679 --> 00:01:09.020
who he's speaking to.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:13.609
And later, when the judge is sentencing Loki
for his crimes against the timeline, he's

00:01:13.609 --> 00:01:18.469
still trying to worm his way out of the situation
and pass the blame on to someone else.

00:01:18.469 --> 00:01:20.200
It's classic Loki.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.390
But the TVA refers to him as "Variant L1130,"
because it's important to remember that the

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:30.310
series isn't following the main version of
Loki, the one who was killed by Thanos in

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:31.369
"Avengers: Infinity War."

00:01:31.369 --> 00:01:36.679
When the Hunters used their reset charge in
Mongolia, they closed the new timeline branch

00:01:36.679 --> 00:01:39.609
Loki created when he escaped with the Tesseract.

00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:44.289
However, it's not entirely clear just how
far the effects of reset charge go.

00:01:44.289 --> 00:01:49.399
As the judge tells Loki during his "trial,"
the Avengers were supposed to go back in time

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:51.369
to retrieve the Infinity Stones.

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:55.509
So just how far back did this particular repair
of the timeline go?

00:01:55.509 --> 00:02:00.189
Regardless of the overall effects of the reset
charge, thanks to the intervention of Agent

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:05.399
Mobius, this mischievous Loki variant is allowed
to live, at least for now.

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:09.330
Owen Wilson is a breath of fresh air in the
MCU as Agent Mobius.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:14.410
He's a welcome addition here as his laidback,
casual nature helps the weirder elements of

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.540
the series go down a little easier.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:21.410
"Loki" introduces Mobius as he's in the middle
of hunting a particularly violent variant

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:27.530
in France in 1549, although the writers are
careful not to mention exactly who this variant

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:28.530
is.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.740
Although the bureaucratic nature of the TVA
might make its employees seem a little cold,

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:37.240
Mobius clearly has a softer side, which we
see when he tries to get more information

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:41.740
from the French child about what happened
in the church and who killed the Hunters.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:46.770
The kid ominously points towards a stained-glass
window depicting the devil, and who else is

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.930
well-known for wearing a set of horns in the
MCU?

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:50.930
You guessed it.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:55.020
Anyway, as entertaining as he is, Mobius has
a crucial role in the show.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.820
He's smoothing over any questions that Loki
(or the viewer) might have about the TVA and

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:00.820
what they do.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:05.260
He's also the stepping stone to understanding
Loki on a psychological level, as he does

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:10.500
a masterful job of getting under his skin
in one of the best scenes in the episode.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.730
When Mobius takes Loki into a room called
the "Time Theater," the pair rewatch some

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:17.390
of the key moments in the God of Mischief's
life.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:20.900
This includes a brilliant scene where Loki
is revealed to have been D.B.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:27.690
Cooper, the real-life man responsible for
hijacking a plane in the 1970s for a $200,000

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.960
ransom before seemingly disappearing.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.760
The answer to this bizarre historical mystery?

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.620
Heimdall pulled Loki out of the sky using
the Bifrost.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:40.750
Yes, that's hilarious, but it's only to catch
audiences off guard as the conversation takes

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:46.850
a much deeper turn and puts Loki's lifelong
motivations under the microscope -- specifically,

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.280
his desire to rule.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:49.640
Why is Loki like this?

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.460
Why does he want to rule Midgard, the Nine
Realms, and Asgard itself?

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.650
The former prince is forced to take a long,
hard look at himself and the ramifications

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:02.230
of his actions, even reckoning with the fact
that he's destined to get his own mother killed

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:06.950
by sending the Dark Elves up a specific set
of stairs in "Thor: The Dark World."

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:08.970
But it all boils down to one question:

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.430
"Do you enjoy hurting people?"

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:16.889
It's only after Loki later realizes the inconsequential
nature of the Infinity Stones within the TVA

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:20.049
that he's completely honest with who he really
is.

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:24.170
By watching his own life, and his brutal death
at the hands of Thanos, play out in the Time

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:27.419
Theater, he finally embraces the truth.

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:32.110
Loki's violence is a "cruel elaborate trick"
to make himself seem like a terrifying person

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:35.940
so people will fear him, even though he himself
thinks he's weak.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:40.930
Oh Loki, you just saved yourself a very expensive
therapist's bill.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:45.389
After Loki's personal growth session, Mobius
reveals the real reason that he's trying to

00:04:45.389 --> 00:04:49.740
work with him: There's another variant of
Loki running around the timeline, killing

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.810
the TVA's Hunters and generally causing chaos.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:56.610
What's the best way of tracking down a vicious
variant of Loki?

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:00.810
Use another variant to get a better insight
into the way the God of Mischief thinks and

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.939
why he constantly engages in this level of
violence.

00:05:03.939 --> 00:05:08.009
The thoughtful conversation Mobius and Loki
had will no doubt come in handy when they

00:05:08.009 --> 00:05:13.180
eventually confront this alternate version
of the Asgardian, if that even is the identity

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.710
of the violent variant.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:20.930
"Loki" Episode 1 then quickly cuts to Salina,
Oklahoma, in 1859, where a group of Hunters

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.650
go searching for someone who's messed with
the timeline and is trying to get rich by

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:26.319
drilling for oil in the past.

00:05:26.319 --> 00:05:28.379
Oh, if only that was the case.

00:05:28.379 --> 00:05:32.529
A hooded figure is revealed to be waiting
for the squad, setting them on fire with the

00:05:32.529 --> 00:05:35.120
oil in the field in a brutal moment.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.241
This could be the same hooded figure who's
briefly seen in the "Loki" trailers, the one

00:05:39.241 --> 00:05:44.749
many people are expecting to be revealed as
Lady Loki, since actress Sophia Di Martino

00:05:44.749 --> 00:05:47.610
has long-been rumored to be playing the character.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:52.350
In the Marvel comics, Loki has taken the form
of a woman from time to time, but it's typically

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.629
been just another form for the same character.

00:05:54.629 --> 00:06:00.009
If the variant the TVA is hunting does turn
out to be Lady Loki, it'll be interesting

00:06:00.009 --> 00:06:05.419
to see how the timeline diverged in a way
to create her, how she escaped, and what she

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:06.419
wants.

00:06:06.419 --> 00:06:09.169
But we'll just have to wait a week to find
out more.

00:06:09.169 --> 00:06:11.460
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.979
Plus, even more Looper videos about Loki are
coming soon.

